{"reference":"John 21","book":"John","chapter":21,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"historical_context","title":"An Unnamed Gospel Witness","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Although this Gospel is traditionally called John, its continuous Greek text does not name its narrator or author. The closing lines identify a disciple who bears witness and wrote these things, but they do not supply that disciple's personal name.","verse_refs":["John 21:24-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_021_01f6b47297f1958a598535d6"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Peter and the Beloved Disciple","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"John 21 reads like an epilogue following the purpose statement in the preceding chapter, though whether it was composed later or by another hand remains disputed. Within this ending, Jesus entrusts Peter with caring for his sheep, speaks of a costly future witness, and redirects Peter away from comparing his path with that of the disciple Jesus loved. The chapter itself does not settle later theories about papal office or rival church factions. Nor does it identify the beloved disciple as John son of Zebedee: that figure remains unnamed, and verse 24 refers to the disciple in the third person.","verse_refs":["John 21:1-25","John 21:15-23","John 21:20-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_021_38befce1cdcdf3209755180d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}